    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2025-2043",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Regional climate modeling is essential for providing reliable information to understand localized impacts and guide adaptation strategies in the context of climate change. This study validates long-term continuous climate simulations over Belgium performed with the ALARO1-SFX model, a novel version of ALARO-1 that incorporates an advanced surface scheme (SURFEX) and improved physiographic datasets. A scale-selective validation setup is introduced, employing a multi-level dynamical downscaling framework to assess the progressive added value of increasing resolution from the mesoscale to convection-permitting scales. The model's performance is evaluated against a gridded observational dataset and precipitation station measurements, focusing on temperature and precipitation biases, diurnal precipitation cycles, and extreme precipitation statistics. Results indicate that higher resolutions (12.5 km and 4 km), combined with the integration of SURFEX into ALARO1-SFX, improve temperature and precipitation biases relative to the 25 km simulation, with the 4 km resolution providing the best representation of hourly extreme precipitation and its diurnal cycle. However, all simulations exhibit varying degrees of wet and cold biases. The findings underscore the added value of convection-permitting modelling for resolving extreme precipitation events and improving diurnal precipitation cycles. Furthermore, the study provides experimental evidence that increasing model resolution adds value for simulating extreme precipitation even when employing a scale-aware deep convection parameterization.",
    "field": "Regional climate modeling",
    "problem": "Existing regional climate models often struggle to accurately represent localized impacts due to coarse resolutions and parameterized convection, leading to biases in temperature, diurnal precipitation cycles, and particularly in the simulation of extreme precipitation events.",
    "method": "This study employs a multi-level dynamical downscaling framework to validate the ALARO1-SFX regional climate model over Belgium, assessing the added value of increasing resolution from mesoscale to convection-permitting scales by comparing simulations against observational data.",
    "task": "The task is to validate the ALARO1-SFX regional climate model by evaluating its performance in simulating long-term climate over Belgium across three different resolutions (25, 12.5, and 4 km) and assessing the added value of increasing resolution.",
